WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:04.210
Film Courage: What is your advice for actors
or comics who are going up against the same

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.490
people time and again [in auditions].

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:09.920
I know of a couple of people who have the
same names on IMDB and it’s been competitive

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.340
when they see each other…it’s awkward.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.220
Jonathan Mangum, Actor/Comedian/Announcer
for the game show Let's Make a Deal: I mean…it’s

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:19.489
competitive if you go into an audition thinking
‘the best person is going to get the job’

00:00:19.489 --> 00:00:20.939
and that is never the case.

00:00:20.939 --> 00:00:24.359
The best person is never the one that gets
the job.

00:00:24.359 --> 00:00:26.109
It’s all about what do they need?

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.109
What type do they need?

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:28.840
Do they need someone who is this tall?

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.669
Does it have to look like the kid they have
already hired?

00:00:31.669 --> 00:00:33.020
Is this guy the friend of the producer?

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:39.040
I mean there are so many variables that it
is never a meritocracy in auditions (ever)

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.039
in my 25 years of doing it.

00:00:42.039 --> 00:00:43.039
There is a level.

00:00:43.039 --> 00:00:47.010
You need to be great to get up into that to
be picked from but there is no “The Best.”

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.920
And as long as you realize there is no “The
Best,” then you can feel less competitive

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:53.489
with people you see all the time.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:57.579
Film Courage: Was it a learning experience,
coming to peace with that?

00:00:57.579 --> 00:01:03.550
Jonathan: Yes, it took a long time because
you’re just like “Ahhh?

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:05.470
They were better than me and got the part.”

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:06.820
It’s like “No.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.040
They may have been better than you but that’s
not why they got the part,” there are so

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:15.140
many reasons that you will never know and
no one calls you and says “Hey, that guy

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:18.430
reminded the producer of his best friend from
high school so they hired him.”

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:24.600
I mean there are so many different variables
it’s ridiculous.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:31.100
You just go in, do your best, bring what you
bring, your special take on it and then once

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.650
you’re done with that, it’s out of your
control.

